100
00:03:24,660 --> 00:03:27,090
We can respond based on the following points.

101
00:03:27,090 --> 00:03:29,770
The first scenario is when developers think the requirement is unreasonable.

102
00:03:29,770 --> 00:03:31,920
Or the requirement itself is overdesigned.

103
00:03:31,920 --> 00:03:32,880
If the requirement is reasonable,

104
00:03:32,880 --> 00:03:33,840
but the developers haven't understood it,

105
00:03:33,840 --> 00:03:36,780
we will present data and the background of the business side.

106
00:03:36,780 --> 00:03:37,590
We will explain the logic,

107
00:03:37,590 --> 00:03:39,430
and if the requirement is indeed overdesigned.

108
00:03:39,430 --> 00:03:40,830
We can develop a better design solution.

109
00:03:40,830 --> 00:03:43,380
Then we can also consider changing the product design approach.

110
00:03:43,380 --> 00:03:45,300
If the second development option isn't feasible.

111
00:03:45,300 --> 00:03:46,820
If the development isn't feasible,

112
00:03:46,820 --> 00:03:48,980
If there's no breakthrough in this industry,

113
00:03:48,980 --> 00:03:50,709
Then we need to put the product plan on hold.

114
00:03:50,709 --> 00:03:53,069
If there is a breakthrough in this industry,

115
00:03:53,069 --> 00:03:55,029
But our company can't implement it,

116
00:03:55,029 --> 00:03:57,580
We can consider outsourcing to third parties.

117
00:03:57,580 --> 00:03:58,740
For example, what I mean is

118
00:03:58,740 --> 00:04:00,060
let's say facial recognition

119
00:04:00,060 --> 00:04:01,380
I don't have an algorithm engineer

120
00:04:01,380 --> 00:04:02,520
I can't do facial recognition myself

121
00:04:02,520 --> 00:04:05,700
but I can definitely buy a third-party service

122
00:04:05,700 --> 00:04:06,860
for a facial recognition system

123
00:04:06,860 --> 00:04:07,780
I call its API

124
00:04:07,780 --> 00:04:10,480
it tells me whether the recognition is successful or not

125
00:04:10,480 --> 00:04:12,980
this is our approach for the second scenario

126
00:04:12,980 --> 00:04:14,640
This is for everyone to understand.

127
00:04:14,640 --> 00:04:16,240
You don't need to give examples to the interviewer.

128
00:04:16,240 --> 00:04:17,279
When you mention these two scenarios,

129
00:04:17,279 --> 00:04:18,200
the interviewer will get it.

130
00:04:18,200 --> 00:04:20,600
The third one is about limited development resources.

131
00:04:20,600 --> 00:04:23,770
When I was working on a marketing project,

132
00:04:23,770 --> 00:04:24,920
I encountered this issue.

133
00:04:24,920 --> 00:04:27,000
I also worked with the developers to sort it out,

134
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:29,280
the priority of the development requests they have on hand.

135
00:04:29,280 --> 00:04:31,000
We've set a schedule for it.

136
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:32,600
This doesn't meet my requirements

137
